I didn't implement the `last-input` recording. The store would need `aap:caps/state-rw`, and the node's `with` clause grants only `http-client`, `notifier` and `wall-clock`. Importing it would exceed the node's authority, so the body doesn't write to any store. Output is unchanged. To get the recording, the node's `with` clause would need a `StateHandle` capability added.

Also, `notified` reports what the notifier actually returned. The body can't force it to `true`, so the `ensures` holds only if the notifier accepts the message.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;
    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::{http_client, notifier};
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        let seconds = wall_clock::now().seconds;

        let fetched = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => body.chars().count() as u32,
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        let message = format!("heartbeat at {}: fetched {} characters", seconds, fetched);
        let notified = notifier::notify(&message);

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

